1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,120
This is KubeSage, an AI SRE team for Kubernetes.

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:05,670
Here is what is new.

3
00:00:06,573 --> 00:00:09,517
KubeSage puts FinOps and SRE in one system.

4
00:00:09,517 --> 00:00:15,473
It plans savings, forecasts rushes from usage and your calendar, and fixes what breaks.

5
00:00:15,473 --> 00:00:19,169
Small models do the legwork, so these runs cost cents.

6
00:00:19,169 --> 00:00:21,428
Every finding must cite evidence.

7
00:00:21,428 --> 00:00:23,345
Agent fixes are git commits.

8
00:00:23,345 --> 00:00:27,863
Plans and fixes reach the cluster only when a person applies them.

9
00:00:28,776 --> 00:00:31,903
Picture a hypothetical store on Azure Kubernetes.

10
00:00:31,903 --> 00:00:36,177
Five services share one node at two hundred eighty dollars a month.

11
00:00:36,177 --> 00:00:39,750
Two services reserve two cores each and sit mostly idle.

12
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:42,111
Checkout peaks every weekday morning.

13
00:00:42,111 --> 00:00:44,535
Black Friday is on the sales calendar.

14
00:00:44,535 --> 00:00:47,916
Tonight, a ledger cache ships with too little memory.

15
00:00:48,826 --> 00:00:51,998
FinOps and engineering keep one business calendar:

16
00:00:51,998 --> 00:00:54,790
working hours, Black Friday, Christmas week.

17
00:00:54,790 --> 00:00:59,992
From it and thirty days of usage, KubeSage forecasts each rush with no model call:

18
00:00:59,992 --> 00:01:02,276
when to pre-warm, and how many pods.

19
00:01:02,276 --> 00:01:05,955
A CPU trigger still catches the flash sale nobody planned.

20
00:01:05,955 --> 00:01:10,586
For Black Friday, the plan asks for a one-day scale-up from seven thirty.

21
00:01:11,492 --> 00:01:13,855
Statistics and the forecast are free.

22
00:01:13,855 --> 00:01:16,602
One Muse Spark call triages every workload.

23
00:01:16,602 --> 00:01:21,903
Spark handles the routine fixes; Muse Wonder takes the hard case and the node pool.

24
00:01:21,903 --> 00:01:26,885
Guardrails floor requests from history and restore dropped calendar pre-warms.

25
00:01:26,885 --> 00:01:32,122
The whole analysis cost twenty-seven cents, under half the price of all on Wonder.

26
00:01:33,027 --> 00:01:37,349
When an alert fires, four small agents gather evidence in parallel.

27
00:01:37,349 --> 00:01:40,640
Findings that cite no collected signal are dropped.

28
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:46,705
A larger Thinker reads the rest, plus your team's plain-English rules, to find the root cause.

29
00:01:46,705 --> 00:01:51,867
An Executor writes the fix as a git commit, after policy checks limit each edit.

30
00:01:53,556 --> 00:01:57,930
In the simulated cluster, each glass shows the CPU a pod reserves.

31
00:01:57,930 --> 00:02:02,967
Analyze plans seventy percent less reservation cost, for twenty-seven cents.

32
00:02:02,967 --> 00:02:08,269
The forecast shows each service's biggest rush, with its pre-warm time and pods.

33
00:02:08,269 --> 00:02:12,776
To fit scheduled peaks, the node pool costs thirty-two percent more.

34
00:02:13,453 --> 00:02:16,936
Apply right-sizes the services and adds the KEDA rules.

35
00:02:16,936 --> 00:02:19,913
It skips moving pods until the new nodes exist.

36
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:21,933
Then the bad release:

37
00:02:21,933 --> 00:02:26,710
ledger-cache runs out of memory, and the ContainerOOMKilled alert fires.

38
00:02:27,326 --> 00:02:28,918
The team starts on its own.

39
00:02:28,918 --> 00:02:33,986
The sub-agents report, the Thinker finds the cause, and the chat shows every hand-off.

40
00:02:34,625 --> 00:02:36,019
The fix is a commit:

41
00:02:36,019 --> 00:02:41,248
more memory, a memory-optimised node pool proposal, and two security fixes.

42
00:02:41,248 --> 00:02:43,130
Sixteen seconds, two cents.

43
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:44,315
A person decides.

44
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:46,052
That is KubeSage:

45
00:02:46,052 --> 00:02:51,150
FinOps and SRE in one system, built for the rush, with a person in charge.
